{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VtPNAdjnRpgFWLfNczoo6s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Lnv4ZGoqmppQ6fuxGfQCsS","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work Review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 Findings submission. Your focus areas are **novelty** and **related work**. You must provide a blind, independent review.\n\n## Venue Scoring Rubric (ACL Rolling Review / EMNLP 2026)\nThree main scores, each 1-5:\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent thoroughness; 4=Strong, sufficient support for all claims; 3=Acceptable, main claims supported but minor gaps; 2=Poor, main claims not sufficiently supported; 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly exciting, would recommend; 4=Exciting; 3=Interesting; 2=Potentially interesting; 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): Composite recommendation. 5=strong accept, 4=accept, 3.5=borderline accept, 3=borderline, 2.5=borderline reject, 2=reject, 1=strong reject\n- **Confidence** (1-5): Your confidence in this review\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Binary Consensus Does Not Improve Circuit Discovery\"\n\nThe paper evaluates whether binary consensus (CSS) — taking the intersection of circuits identified by multiple automated circuit discovery methods — improves circuit quality. It tests CSS across 6 task×model configurations (4 tasks on GPT-2 Small, 1 task on Qwen-0.5B) using 3 methods (EAP-IG, Edge Pruning, ACDC). Main findings: 0/6 configurations show statistically significant positive CSS advantage. Two mechanistic failure modes are identified: (1) ACDC's isinf binarization collapses consensus to a single method's output, (2) component-level scoring on large models produces near-zero inter-method overlap.\n\n## Paper Content Detail\n\n**Abstract**: Tests CSS across 4 tasks on GPT-2 Small and 1 on Qwen-0.5B. K=3 methods (EAP-IG, EP, ACDC). 0/6 significant positive ΔCPR. Claims \"consensus-as-validation paradigm is fundamentally flawed for current circuit discovery methods.\"\n\n**Section 1 - Introduction**: Frames the problem as: practitioners face divergent circuits, consensus is the natural response. Positions CSS as the first systematic test of this intuition. Claims threefold contribution: (1) first systematic negative evaluation, (2) mechanistic explanations, (3) evidence against the community's implicit assumption.\n\n**Section 2 - Related Work** (short, ~0.5 page):\n- Circuit discovery methods: ACDC (Conmy 2023), EAP-IG (Syed 2023), EP (Li 2024), IFR, PIE (Zhang 2026)\n- Multi-method consensus: Nogueira & Brown 2016 (feature selection stability), Kuncheva & Whitaker 2003 (ensemble diversity). Mondorf et al. 2025 (continuous score averaging). CIRCUS (Parekh et al. 2026, within-method threshold diversity)\n- The consensus assumption in practice: argues community uses multi-method agreement as informal validation without testing it\n\n**Section 3 - CSS Framework**: Formal definition of CSS(τ), CPR metric, D025 decision gate with 4 conditions\n\n**Section 5 - Results**: \n- 0/6 PASS on D025 gate across all configs\n- Two mechanistic failure modes: binarization pathology (§5.3), component-level scoring collapse (§5.7)\n- Size-controlled analysis dissolves IOI apparent success (§5.4)\n- Bootstrap CIs: 0/6 significant positive, 3/6 significant negative\n\n**Section 6 - Discussion**:\n- \"Why negative results matter here\" — argues non-trivial because community actively uses consensus\n- \"The independence illusion\" — diversity+accuracy argument from ensemble theory\n- Comparison with Mondorf et al. continuous averaging — complementary\n- CIRCUS within-method baseline also fails\n- Limitations: 2 model scales, CPR-only, K≈2 effective diversity\n\n**Section 7 - Conclusion**: Binary consensus universally fails, challenges informal validation practice\n\n**References**: 9 total — Conmy 2023, Kuncheva 2003, Li 2024, Mondorf 2025, Nogueira 2016, Parekh 2026, Syed 2023, Wang 2023, Zhang 2026\n\n## Key Related Work Context\n- Mondorf et al. 2025 propose continuous score averaging as alternative to binary consensus — paper acknowledges but argues it doesn't resolve Failure Mode 2\n- CIRCUS (Parekh et al. 2026) explores within-method threshold diversity — paper claims it also produces negative CPR\n- PIE (Zhang et al. 2026) proposes pruning iterative ensembles — only cited, not tested\n- The paper has only 9 references for an 8-page EMNLP paper\n\n## YOUR TASK\nProvide a detailed review focusing on **novelty** and **related work**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Novelty of the research question**: Is testing binary consensus in circuit discovery genuinely novel and important? Does the community actually rely on this assumption, or is the paper attacking a straw man?\n2. **Positioning against prior work**: Is the distinction from Mondorf et al. 2025 and CIRCUS (Parekh 2026) clearly drawn? Are there missing related works?\n3. **Strength of the \"community assumption\" claim**: Does the paper provide sufficient evidence that the community actually uses binary consensus as validation? Are there citations to papers that do this?\n4. **Contribution significance**: Is a negative result on 6 configurations with 3 methods sufficient for a venue like EMNLP Findings? Are the mechanistic explanations (binarization pathology, scoring collapse) genuinely insightful or obvious?\n5. **Scope of claims vs. evidence**: The title says \"Binary Consensus Does Not Improve Circuit Discovery\" — is this supported by testing only K=3 specific methods on 2 models? Is the generalization warranted?\n6. **Related work completeness**: Only 9 references seems thin. Are key works in circuit discovery, ensemble methods, or interpretability validation missing?\n7. **Framing as \"fundamentally flawed\"**: Is this overclaiming given the evidence scope?\n8. **Writing quality and presentation**: Is the paper clearly written? Are claims appropriately hedged?\n\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## Summary of Contributions (2-3 sentences)\n## Strengths (bulleted)\n## Weaknesses (bulleted)\n## Questions for Authors\n## Minor Issues\n## Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous. Evaluate whether the novelty and positioning are sufficient for EMNLP 2026 Findings."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2000,"cache_read_input_tokens":57069,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2000},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92ec98e3-e572-42db-9ebd-25eabdb3cb35","uuid":"366caf06-6330-476c-a6a3-043850d7acff"}
